# Approach 2: Simple Observation

# Complexity
- Time complexity: $$O(n)$$

- Space complexity: $$O(1)$$
- 
# Code
```java []
class Solution {
    public int largestInteger(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int maxResult = -1;

        //only one subarray possible. Select the largest element
        if (k == n) {
            for (int num : nums) {
                maxResult = Math.max(maxResult, num);
            }
            return maxResult;
        }

        int[] freq = new int[51];
        for (int num : nums) {
            freq[num]++;
        }

        //every index is one subarray. Find largest element which appears only once
        if (k == 1) {
            for (int i = 50; i >= 0; i--) {
                if (freq[i] == 1) {
                    return i;
                }
            }
            return -1;
        }

        //Notice that only the first and last element are going to be the ones which will appear only in one subarray. Others will be part of more than one subarray always
        if (k > 1 && k < n) {
            if (freq[nums[0]] == 1) {
                maxResult = Math.max(maxResult, nums[0]);
            }

            if (freq[nums[n - 1]] == 1) {
                maxResult = Math.max(maxResult, nums[n - 1]);
            }
        }

        return maxResult;
    }
}
```
